SLUG = {2: "schwa_bytes", 3: "null", 4: "count", 5: "index", 6: "explain_plan", 7: "join", 8: "deadlock", 9: "bind_variables", 10: "undo", 11: "flashback", 12: "partitioning", 13: "parallel_query", 14: "materialized_views", 15: "optimizer_statistics", 16: "bind_peeking", 17: "sql_plan_baselines", 18: "redo_commit", 19: "result_cache", 20: "fragmentation_shrink"}
FOLLOW = "Follow S Q L developer, number one!"

N = {
2: [
 "Last time we asked: how many bytes does the letter schwa take? The letter A takes one byte. Schwa takes two! Why twice as much?",
 "The ASCII table holds only one hundred and twenty eight characters. Latin letters, digits, and symbols. There's simply no room for schwa.",
 "The solution is Unicode. It gives every letter in the world its own number. Schwa is number six hundred and one. But one byte can only hold up to two hundred and fifty five. So schwa needs two bytes.",
 "In UTF-8, schwa is stored as these two bytes. The leading bits, one one zero, say: I am a two byte character. And one zero says: I am a continuation.",
 "Let's check it in Oracle. LENGTH counts characters, and returns one. LENGTHB counts bytes, and returns two.",
 "Here's a real banking bug. A column defined as VARCHAR2, four BYTE. Insert Baku, written with the Azerbaijani dotless i, and you get ORA twelve eight nine nine. Four letters, but five bytes. The fix: define it as four CHAR.",
 "Remember: each of these letters takes two bytes. " + FOLLOW,
],
3: [
 "What does this query return? Zero rows. No error, but no result either. Why?",
 "Because NULL doesn't mean empty. NULL means unknown. Look at these two closed boxes. Do they hold the same thing? We don't know!",
 "SQL has three answers: true, false, and unknown. Five equals five is true. Five equals three is false. Five equals NULL is unknown. And even NULL equals NULL is unknown.",
 "And WHERE only lets true through. False is blocked. Unknown is blocked too. So phone equals NULL always returns zero rows.",
 "The right way is IS NULL. It doesn't compare, it checks. And now we get our three clients.",
 "Now the most dangerous trap: NOT IN with a NULL. This returns zero clients! Oracle reads it as: id not equal to one, and id not equal to two, and id not equal to NULL. True and unknown gives unknown. So your report is silently empty.",
 "Remember three rules. Use IS NULL instead of equals NULL. Use NOT EXISTS instead of NOT IN. And use NVL to give a default value. Bonus: in Oracle, an empty string is also NULL.",
 "Next video: COUNT star versus COUNT column. " + FOLLOW,
],
4: [
 "Same table, two queries. COUNT star returns five. COUNT phone returns three. Which one is right? Both!",
 "COUNT star counts every row. It doesn't matter if a row contains NULLs. If the row exists, it's counted. Five.",
 "COUNT of a column counts only non NULL values. Leyla and Nigar have no phone, so they are skipped. Three, not five.",
 "So here's the rule. COUNT star counts rows. COUNT column counts non NULL values. COUNT DISTINCT counts unique non NULL values. And a myth: COUNT one is not faster. In Oracle, it's exactly the same as COUNT star.",
 "Now the hidden trap: AVG. Four employees, two bonuses are NULL. AVG ignores the NULLs, and divides three hundred by two. One hundred fifty. Wrong! With NVL, NULL becomes zero. Three hundred divided by four is seventy five. Now the report is correct.",
 "Here's a handy trick. How many clients have no phone? COUNT star minus COUNT phone. Five minus three, two.",
 "Remember: aggregates skip NULLs. When you write a report, always ask: can this column be NULL?",
 "Next video: how does an index work? " + FOLLOW,
],
5: [
 "One million clients. Find client number seven seven seven seven seven seven. Without an index: ten thousand blocks. With an index: just four. Where does this difference come from?",
 "Imagine a five hundred page book. Which page mentions Baku? You could flip page by page, or use the index at the back: word, page number. Straight to page three forty two. A database index works exactly like this.",
 "Without an index, Oracle does a full table scan. The table is stored in blocks, and the rows are not sorted. Oracle doesn't know where our client is, so it reads every single block from disk. Even after finding it, the scan keeps going.",
 "So what is an index? A separate, sorted list: the key, plus an address called ROWID. The ROWID is the row's physical address: object, file, block, and row. Just like a postal address.",
 "Inside, the index is a B-tree. We're searching for seven seven seven seven seven seven. At the root: it's bigger than six hundred sixty six thousand, so go right. One block. At the branch: smaller than seven hundred eighty thousand, go to the middle leaf. Two blocks. At the leaf: found it, together with its ROWID. Three blocks. Then, using the ROWID, jump straight to the table block. Just four blocks, no searching.",
 "Ten thousand blocks versus four. That's about two and a half thousand times less reading. And a B-tree is usually only three or four levels deep, even for huge tables.",
 "Creating one is simple: create index on clients, client number. Now check the execution plan. Before: table access full. After: index range scan.",
 "But careful. An index can exist and still not be used. Trap one: a function on the column, like UPPER. Trap two: comparing a text column with a number, which adds a hidden TO NUMBER. Trap three: LIKE with a leading percent sign. Trap four: IS NULL, because a B-tree doesn't store entirely null keys.",
 "To make it even faster: use composite indexes with the right column order. Use covering indexes, so Oracle never touches the table. And index columns with high selectivity, with many unique values.",
 "But indexes are not free. Every insert, update, and delete must also update the index. So don't index every column.",
 "Remember: an index is a sorted list plus ROWID. A B-tree finds rows in three or four steps. And functions or leading wildcards switch it off. Next video: how to read an execution plan. " + FOLLOW,
],
6: [
 "Is your query slow? Oracle can tell you why. You just need to know how to read the plan.",
 "To see the plan, write EXPLAIN PLAN FOR, followed by your query. Then select from DBMS X PLAN dot DISPLAY. In SQL Developer, just press F10. It doesn't run the query, so it's always safe.",
 "Four columns matter. Operation: what Oracle does in this step. Name: which table or index. Rows: how many rows Oracle expects. It's only an estimate. And Cost: a relative score for comparing plans. It is not seconds!",
 "Which step runs first? Not the top one! The deepest step runs first. Step one: find the client in the index. Two: fetch the client row by ROWID. Three: find the client's accounts in the index. Four: read the accounts. Five: nested loops joins them. Six: return the result.",
 "Learn to recognize operations. Green is good: index unique scan, index range scan, access by ROWID. Yellow depends on context: nested loops, hash join, sort. Red needs attention: full scans on big tables, and merge join cartesian. Still, a full scan isn't always bad.",
 "Here's a bad plan. A full scan reads five million account rows, but we need only three. The fix: create an index on the join column. Now the plan uses nested loops with index range scans, and the cost drops from twelve thousand to just seven.",
 "Don't miss the predicate section. Access means rows were found directly through the index. Filter means rows were checked after reading them. And here's that hidden TO NUMBER from the last video. The plan exposes it!",
 "The optimizer can be wrong. Use the gather plan statistics hint to compare estimated and actual rows. Estimated: one. Actual: two hundred fifty thousand! The statistics are stale. Gather fresh statistics, and the optimizer will choose a better plan.",
 "Your checklist. Full scans on big tables? Are the row estimates realistic? Access or filter? And where does the cost jump?",
 "Next video: join types. Nested loops versus hash join. " + FOLLOW,
],
7: [
 "You write a JOIN. But how does Oracle actually combine two tables? There are three algorithms, and the wrong choice turns seconds into hours.",
 "Here's our query: clients joined with accounts. Three clients, and five million accounts.",
 "Method one: nested loops. Take one row from the outer table, then use the index to find its matches in the inner table. Ali: three accounts. Leyla: one account. Murad: two accounts. Three clients means just three index lookups. When the outer side is small, nested loops is the fastest.",
 "But what if the outer table has a million rows? That's a million index lookups. Very slow. For big data sets, Oracle uses something else.",
 "Method two: hash join. Phase one, build. Each row of the small table is hashed and dropped into a bucket, like numbered hooks in a cloakroom. Phase two, probe. The big table is read once. For each row, compute the hash, and look straight into its bucket. No searching. Each table is read only once, but it only works with equality, and the hash table lives in memory.",
 "Method three: merge join. Two sorted lists, joined like a zipper. The pointers only move forward, never back. Both sides must be sorted first, but it also works with greater than, less than, and between.",
 "So which one, when? Nested loops for small sets with an index. Hash join for big, unsorted sets with equality. Merge join for sorted data and range conditions.",
 "Who decides? The optimizer, based on row estimates. If the estimate is wrong, the join is wrong. Hints exist, but they are a last resort. Fix the statistics first.",
 "Next video: deadlocks, when two transactions wait for each other. " + FOLLOW,
],
8: [
 "Two money transfers at the same time. Suddenly, Oracle throws ORA zero zero zero sixty: deadlock detected. What happened? Two transactions are waiting for each other forever.",
 "First, the basics. An UPDATE locks the row. Session A updates account one oh one, and Oracle locks it. Session B wants the same row, so it must wait. When A commits, the lock is released, and B can continue.",
 "Now, two transfers at the same time. A moves money from account one oh one to two oh two. B moves money from two oh two to one oh one. Step one: A locks one oh one. Step two: B locks two oh two. Step three: A needs two oh two, so it waits. Step four: B needs one oh one, so it waits too.",
 "Deadlock! A waits for B, and B waits for A. Nobody can move. It's like two cars meeting on a narrow bridge: if neither backs up, both wait forever.",
 "Luckily, Oracle detects this by itself, within a few seconds. One session's statement fails with ORA zero zero zero sixty. But careful: only that last statement is undone, not the whole transaction. B still holds its lock, so your code must roll back. Then A gets the account, and finishes.",
 "Fix number one: always lock in the same order, lower ID first. Both sessions try to lock one oh one first. B waits at step one, but it holds nothing, so there's no cycle. A finishes and commits, then B continues smoothly.",
 "Fix number two: limit the waiting. Select for update no wait fails instantly with ORA zero zero fifty four, instead of hanging. Or use wait five, to wait at most five seconds. And keep transactions short: commit quickly, and never wait for a user inside a transaction.",
 "And a hidden cause: an unindexed foreign key. When you delete a client, Oracle must check the accounts table for related rows. Without an index on client I D, it locks the whole accounts table, and other sessions get stuck. The fix: always index your foreign key columns.",
 "Remember: same lock order, short transactions, no endless waits, index your foreign keys, and on ORA zero zero zero sixty, roll back and retry.",
 "Next video: bind variables, hard parse versus soft parse. " + FOLLOW,
],
9: [
 "The same query runs ten thousand times, and Oracle learns it from scratch ten thousand times. The problem isn't the query itself. It's how you write it.",
 "When your SQL arrives, Oracle does three things. Parse: check the syntax, tables, and privileges. Optimize: pick the best execution plan. And execute. Doing all three is called a hard parse, and the first two steps are the expensive ones.",
 "So Oracle keeps ready plans in memory, in the shared pool. The key is the exact text of the query. The first time, the text isn't found, so it's a hard parse, and the plan is cached. The second time, the same text is found. That's a soft parse. The plan is ready, and it's very fast.",
 "Now the problem: literal values. Where I D equals one oh one, one oh two, one oh three. Each number changes the text, so each query gets a new hash, and it's never found. Every single one is a hard parse, and the memory fills up with copies of the same query.",
 "The fix: bind variables. Where I D equals colon I D. The text is always the same, and only the value is passed separately. One hard parse, and every other execution is a soft parse. One plan, thousands of uses.",
 "Ten thousand queries: ten thousand hard parses with literals, versus just one with binds. Less CPU, a clean shared pool, and a system that scales. In a busy bank, that's the difference between a stalled system and a smooth one.",
 "And there's a security bonus. If you build dynamic SQL by concatenating text, user input becomes part of the SQL. Pass x, quote, or one equals one, and you get every client back. That's SQL injection. With a bind variable and the USING clause, a value always stays a value. It's never executed as code.",
 "Good news for PL/SQL developers: in static PL/SQL, variables are bound automatically. The risk comes from dynamic SQL built with concatenation, and from application code in Java, dot net, or Python. Setting cursor sharing to force is only an emergency patch, not a real fix.",
 "Remember: literals mean a hard parse every time. Binds mean one plan, many uses. The shared pool looks up by exact text. Concatenation means injection risk. And in dynamic SQL, use USING.",
 "Next video: the undo secret. Why readers never block writers in Oracle. " + FOLLOW,
],
10: [
 "You're reading a report, and at the same moment, someone changes a balance. Which number will your report see? The answer is one of Oracle's best secrets: undo.",
 "Here's the problem. The writer sets the balance to five hundred, but hasn't committed yet. At the same moment, the reader selects the balance. Should it see five hundred? No, that's uncommitted data. Should it wait? No, then everyone blocks everyone. Oracle's answer: the reader sees the last committed value, one thousand. In Oracle, readers don't block writers, and writers don't block readers.",
 "So what happens during an update? First, the old value, one thousand, is copied to the undo segment. Then, the new value, five hundred, is written to the data block, and the row is locked by the writer. Undo is simply the state before the change. That's the whole secret.",
 "Now, what does the reader see? Its query started at S C N one hundred. The writer changed the block at S C N one oh five. When the reader reaches the block, it notices the change happened after its own start. So Oracle rebuilds the old version from undo, and returns one thousand. No waiting. Every query sees a snapshot of the moment it started.",
 "This matters a lot in banking. A report sums ten accounts, one thousand each, ten thousand in total. In the middle of the scan, three hundred moves from account nine to account two. Account two was already read, account nine was not. A naive read would count nine thousand seven hundred. Oracle reads account nine as of the query start, from undo, and the total stays exactly ten thousand.",
 "Undo has a second job: rollback. When the writer rolls back, Oracle simply takes the old value from undo and writes it back. Even after a crash, uncommitted work is undone the same way.",
 "But here's the trap. Undo isn't infinite. It's a circular buffer, and once a change is committed, its old value can be overwritten after a while. If a long query still needs that old version, it may already be gone. The result: ORA zero fifteen fifty five, snapshot too old.",
 "How to avoid it? Keep old values longer with undo retention and a bigger undo tablespace. Speed up the long query. And don't commit inside a loop while you're still fetching from the same big cursor. That's the most common cause.",
 "Remember: an update copies the old value to undo. Readers never wait for writers. Each query sees a snapshot of its start. Rollback restores from undo. And ORA zero fifteen fifty five means the undo is already gone.",
 "Next video: flashback query. Time travel with AS OF TIMESTAMP. " + FOLLOW,
],
11: [
 "It's five past two. Someone accidentally sets every balance to zero, and commits. The backup is from last night. Can we get the data back? Yes. Oracle remembers the past.",
 "Think of it as Oracle's time machine. Slide back to two o'clock, and you see the table exactly as it was, even though the change was committed. It's not magic. It's undo. So let's look inside.",
 "Here's the mechanism. The query asks for the state as of two o'clock. First, Oracle converts that time into its internal clock, the S C N. Two o'clock is S C N five thousand. Then it reads the data block. The value there was changed at S C N fifty two hundred, which is newer than the target. So Oracle follows the pointer into undo, finds the older version, and returns the result.",
 "That pointer is the key. Every change keeps a link to the previous version, and together they form a chain. Oracle starts at the block: zero, at S C N fifty two hundred. Too new, so step back. Next: twelve hundred, at S C N forty nine hundred. That's before the target. Found it. At two o'clock, the balance was twelve hundred.",
 "Now restoring is easy. Read the past state, and write it back into the current table. Each account gets its two o'clock value back. One statement can restore a thousand accounts, without touching a backup.",
 "This is a whole family, built around undo. Flashback query, with AS OF, shows one moment. Versions query shows a row's full change history. Flashback table rewinds an entire table, and needs row movement enabled. And a dropped table comes back from the recycle bin, not from undo.",
 "But there's a limit. You can only go back as far as undo is kept, the undo retention window. Step outside it, and the old version is gone: ORA zero fifteen fifty five. Truncate writes no undo, so there's no way back. And if the table's structure changed, you get ORA zero fourteen sixty six.",
 "In banking, this is gold. Recover a wrong update or delete in minutes. Answer an auditor: what was this balance yesterday at six P M? Or compare data before and after an end of day batch. Just remember: flashback is a seatbelt, not a replacement for backups.",
 "Remember: AS OF shows a past moment. The S C N is Oracle's internal clock. The undo chain holds the old versions. Versions shows the full history. And the undo window is your limit.",
 "Next video: partitioning. How to split billions of rows into pieces. " + FOLLOW,
],
12: [
 "Two billion transactions. You only need March. But without partitioning, Oracle reads all of history, row after row, just to find one month.",
 "Partitioning splits one huge table into smaller physical pieces. Here, one partition per month, each stored separately. But for your SQL, nothing changes. It's still one table called transactions.",
 "So where does a new row go? Every partitioned table has a partition key. Here, it's the transaction date. Oracle reads the key of each new row, and routes it to the right partition automatically. March goes to March. July to July. December to December.",
 "Now the magic: partition pruning. Your query asks for March only. The optimizer knows which partitions can possibly hold that data, and skips all the others. Instead of twelve partitions, Oracle reads just one. In the execution plan, you'll see partition range single.",
 "There are three main ways to split. Range: each partition holds a range of values, perfect for dates. List: each partition holds specific values, like regions or branches. And hash: Oracle spreads rows evenly using a hash of the key, like account I D, so there are no hot spots.",
 "Partitioning also gives you lifecycle superpowers. With interval partitioning, Oracle creates next month's partition by itself, as soon as data arrives. And archiving old data? A delete of three hundred million rows can take hours, generating undo, redo and locks. Dropping the whole partition takes about a second.",
 "Indexes come in two flavors. A local index is split exactly like the table, so every partition has its own small index. A global index covers all partitions at once. When you drop a partition, the local index simply goes with it. But a global index becomes unusable, unless you add update global indexes to the operation.",
 "And here's the trap. Wrap the partition key in a function, like to char, and the optimizer can't map it to partitions anymore. All twelve partitions are scanned, partition range all. The same happens when the partition key isn't in your where clause at all. The fix: compare the raw column with a date range, and pruning is back.",
 "Remember: the partition key routes every row. Pruning reads only what you need. Pick range, list or hash by the shape of your data. Drop partition is an instant archive. And a function on the key switches pruning off. One note: partitioning is a separately licensed enterprise option.",
 "Next video: parallel query. One query, many workers. " + FOLLOW,
],
13: [
 "A ten gigabyte report, and one single process reading every block, one by one. Forty minutes. What if eight workers shared the job?",
 "That's parallel query. Your session becomes the query coordinator, the boss of the operation. It recruits parallel server processes to do the heavy lifting. How many? That's the D O P, the degree of parallelism. You can request it with a hint, or let Oracle choose automatically.",
 "First, the coordinator cuts the table into granules, small ranges of blocks. Every free worker grabs the next granule. When it's done, it grabs another. Faster workers simply take more, and nobody waits for a slow teammate. That's dynamic load balancing.",
 "For joins and group by, Oracle uses two teams. Producers scan the data, and redistribute each row by its key through table queues. Each consumer receives all the rows for its keys, does its part of the work, and sends the result to the coordinator. That's why a D O P of four can actually use eight processes: two teams of four.",
 "Finally, the coordinator assembles the answer. Each worker computes a partial sum over its own granules. One hundred twenty, ninety five, one hundred thirty, one hundred five million. The coordinator adds them up, and returns one final result.",
 "So how much faster is it? In this example, forty minutes drop to twenty one, then eleven, then six. But it's never perfectly linear. At some point the C P Us or the disks are saturated, and going from eight to sixteen gives you almost nothing.",
 "When should you use it? Parallel query shines on big full scans and reports, end of day batch jobs, index builds, and data warehouse queries. But it's bad for short O L T P queries, single row lookups, or hundreds of users at once. For tiny queries, starting the workers costs more than the work itself.",
 "And here's the trap. One parallel query is fast. Six sessions all asking for a D O P of sixteen is a traffic jam. The C P Us are saturated, everyone slows down, and Oracle runs out of parallel servers, so queries get downgraded or wait in a queue. Control it centrally with resource manager and automatic D O P, not with hints everywhere.",
 "Remember: the coordinator is your session. The parallel servers are the workers. Granules give dynamic load balancing. Joins use two teams, producers and consumers. And parallel query is for big work, not for O L T P.",
 "Next video: materialized views. Answers computed in advance. " + FOLLOW,
],
14: [
 "A dashboard shows total balances per branch. Five hundred users open it, and every click re-calculates the same answer from two billion rows. Again, and again, and again.",
 "A normal view is just a saved query. Every select runs the whole thing over the base tables again. A materialized view is different: it stores the result itself, physically, like a real table. A view is the recipe. A materialized view is the meal, already cooked.",
 "Here's how it's built. The materialized view query joins and aggregates the base tables once. Two billion transactions go through the group by, and the tiny result, just four rows, is stored on disk. Now five hundred users read four rows instead of crunching two billion.",
 "But data keeps changing. New transactions keep arriving in the base table, and the materialized view doesn't know about them. The real total for Baku is now nine hundred twenty five million, but the view still says nine twenty. Its numbers are stale. So how, and when, do we refresh it?",
 "Option one: complete refresh. Oracle throws away the materialized view's contents, and runs the whole query again, over all two billion rows. It's always correct, but expensive, so it usually runs at night, or after end of day.",
 "Option two: fast refresh. A materialized view log on the base table records every change, like a small journal. Plus five million for Baku, plus two for Ganja, minus one for Sumgait. A fast refresh reads only that journal, and applies the deltas. No full scan. Seconds instead of minutes.",
 "And when should it refresh? On commit means the view is refreshed as part of every commit on the base table. It's always fresh, but every commit gets slower. On demand means you decide, for example a scheduled job after end of day. Pick based on how fresh the numbers must be, and how busy your system is.",
 "And here's the real magic: query rewrite. The user writes a normal query against the base table. With enable query rewrite, the optimizer notices that a materialized view already holds the answer, and silently reads the view instead. In the plan you'll see mat view rewrite access full. Same S Q L, no code change.",
 "Remember: a view is a saved query. A materialized view is a saved result. Complete refresh recomputes everything. Fast refresh with a log applies only the changes. And query rewrite lets the optimizer use the view for you.",
 "Next video: optimizer statistics. How Oracle guesses row counts. " + FOLLOW,
],
15: [
 "Same query, same code. Yesterday it ran in two seconds. Today, twenty minutes. What changed? Nothing, except the optimizer's guess about your data.",
 "Think of the optimizer as a GPS. Your S Q L can run in many ways, and each way is a route, an execution plan. The optimizer estimates the cost of every route, and picks the cheapest one. But every estimate comes from its map of your data. That map is the statistics.",
 "So what's inside the map? Table statistics: how many rows and blocks the table has. Column statistics: how many distinct values, how many nulls, and the lowest and highest value. And index statistics: how deep the tree is, and how well the table is ordered for that index.",
 "Here's how Oracle estimates rows. Where status equals frozen. Without extra information, it uses a simple formula: number of rows divided by number of distinct values. One million rows, four statuses, so it expects two hundred fifty thousand rows. But that assumes every value appears equally often.",
 "Reality is skewed. Almost all accounts are active. Only one thousand are frozen. The optimizer still expects two hundred fifty thousand, so it picks a full scan. In reality, an index lookup would be about two hundred fifty times better. Wrong estimate, wrong plan.",
 "The fix is a histogram. A histogram stores how often each value really appears. Now the optimizer knows that frozen is rare, estimates one thousand rows, and chooses an index range scan. Same S Q L, right plan.",
 "But the silent killer is stale statistics. The map says one million rows, analyzed three months ago. After a migration, the table really has fifty million. The optimizer confidently plans for a small table, and picks nested loops over fifty million rows. That's how two seconds become twenty minutes.",
 "So keep the map fresh. By default, Oracle regathers statistics in a nightly maintenance window, for every object where more than ten percent of the rows changed. But after a huge load or an end of day batch, don't wait for the night: gather statistics right away with D B M S stats. And don't use the old analyze command for this.",
 "Remember: the optimizer is a GPS that picks the cheapest estimate. Statistics are its map. The basic formula assumes an even spread. Histograms handle skewed data. And stale statistics turn two seconds into twenty minutes.",
 "Next video: bind peeking. One plan for every value? " + FOLLOW,
],
16: [
 "One S Q L, two users. For frozen accounts it's fast. For active accounts, it crawls. Same text means the same plan, and that's exactly the problem.",
 "Two good ideas collide here. Bind variables give you one shared cursor and one plan, reused by everyone. Histograms tell the optimizer that rare and common values may need different plans. Put them together, and Oracle has to choose: which value decides the plan?",
 "The answer is bind peeking. On the very first execution, the cursor is hard parsed, and the optimizer peeks at the actual bind value, as if it were a literal. The value is frozen. The histogram says that's only one thousand rows. So it builds an index range scan, and caches that plan in the shared pool, for everyone.",
 "And then everyone reuses it. Frozen: fast. Closed: still fine. But active means almost a million single row index lookups, instead of one full scan. Painfully slow. The plan depends on who came first, so a restart on a Monday morning can flip it completely.",
 "Since Oracle eleven g, there's adaptive cursor sharing. Because the column has a histogram, the cursor is marked bind sensitive, and Oracle starts watching it. When it sees row counts like one thousand versus nine hundred seventy thousand, the cursor becomes bind aware.",
 "Now one S Q L can have several plans. Oracle keeps separate child cursors. For every execution, it peeks at the selectivity of the value, and routes it to the right child. Rare values get the index plan. Common values get the full scan. Everyone is fast.",
 "But it learns after the pain. Adaptive cursor sharing reacts, it doesn't predict. It needs at least one badly performing execution before it creates a better plan. And if the cursor is flushed from the shared pool, the learning starts again. Still, much better than one wrong plan forever.",
 "Your toolbox. Check V dollar S Q L: is the cursor bind sensitive, is it bind aware, and how many children does it have? Use S Q L plan baselines to lock in a proven good plan, so a bad peek can't take over. And think about your data: for a column with only a handful of very skewed values, sometimes a literal is the honest choice.",
 "Remember: with bind peeking, the first value decides. The cached plan is reused for everyone. With skewed data, some values suffer. Adaptive cursor sharing creates a child cursor per range. And baselines lock in a good plan.",
 "Next video: S Q L plan baselines. Freeze a good plan forever. " + FOLLOW,
],
17: [
 "Friday, your end of day query runs in two seconds. Over the weekend, statistics are refreshed, or the database is upgraded. Monday: forty minutes. The plan flipped. What if Oracle could only use plans you've approved?",
 "That's S Q L plan management. It adds a gatekeeper between the optimizer and execution. For each S Q L statement, Oracle keeps a baseline: the set of accepted plans. The optimizer can still build a plan, but it may only run a plan that's in the baseline.",
 "So how do plans get in? The common way: load the plan that runs well today, straight from the cursor cache, with D B M S S P M. Or let Oracle capture baselines automatically for statements that run repeatedly. The first plan that enters the baseline is accepted. That's your known good plan.",
 "Now the statistics change, and the optimizer prefers a new plan, plan B. But plan B isn't accepted. It's stored in the plan history for later, and it is not executed. Oracle runs the accepted plan A instead. Your end of day still runs in two seconds.",
 "But what if plan B is actually better? That's what evolution is for. Oracle test drives the new plan and the baseline plan, and compares their real performance. Only if the new plan is clearly faster does it become accepted. Since Oracle twelve c, an automatic evolve task does this every night in the maintenance window.",
 "To see it working, query D B A S Q L plan baselines: each plan shows whether it's enabled, accepted, and fixed. The execution plan's note section tells you when a baseline was used. And mark a plan as fixed when you never want anything else for that statement.",
 "Here's a bank playbook for upgrades. Before the upgrade, capture baselines for your critical end of day statements. Upgrade, patch, or refresh statistics. Oracle keeps using the same accepted plans, so there are no surprises. Then let evolution accept only plans that prove they're faster.",
 "One trap: a baseline stores how to build a plan, not the result itself. If you drop an index that plan A depends on, Oracle can't reproduce it, and the optimizer quietly falls back to its own new plan. So keep the objects your baselines depend on, and recheck them after schema changes.",
 "Remember: a baseline is a set of accepted plans. A new plan goes to the history, not to execution. Evolution accepts it only if it's faster. Fixed pins one plan. And before upgrades, capture your critical S Q L.",
 "Next video: redo and commit. Why commit is so fast. " + FOLLOW,
],
18: [
 "Updating a million rows takes thirty seconds. But the commit? A hundredth of a second. Why is commit so fast, and how does a power cut lose nothing? The answer is redo.",
 "When you update a row, Oracle changes the block in the buffer cache, in memory. Nothing is written to the table on disk yet. The old value is saved in undo, for rollback and for other readers. And a tiny redo record, describing the change, goes into the redo log buffer.",
 "Undo versus redo. Two journals, opposite directions. Undo stores the old value, so Oracle can go back: rollback, and consistent reads. Redo stores the change itself, so Oracle can go forward and replay it after a crash. Undo lives in the undo tablespace, redo in the redo log files. Fun fact: even writing undo generates redo, so undo survives a crash too.",
 "Now you commit. The log writer, L G W R, writes the redo buffer to the online redo log on disk. It's a small, sequential write, and as soon as it's on disk, your commit returns. The changed table block is still only in memory. And that's perfectly safe.",
 "The dirty data blocks are written later, by the database writer, D B W R. It writes in big batches at checkpoints, completely independent of your commit. Fast sequential redo on commit, slow random datafile writes in the background. That's the trick.",
 "Now, the power goes out. Everything in memory is gone. On restart, Oracle rolls forward: it replays the redo log and re-applies every change since the last checkpoint. Then it rolls back anything that was never committed, using undo. Nothing committed is lost.",
 "Redo logs work in a circle. There are several online log groups. L G W R fills one, then switches to the next, and eventually overwrites the oldest. In archive log mode, each full log is copied away first. That's the foundation of your backups, point in time recovery, and Data Guard.",
 "So how often should you commit? Every commit waits for a disk write, the log file sync wait. Commit every row, and you wait a million times. But never committing a huge batch is bad too: undo grows and locks are held for hours. Commit at the end of a logical unit of work, or in sensible batches.",
 "Remember: an update changes memory. Undo keeps the old value, redo keeps the change. Commit means L G W R writes redo. After a crash, Oracle replays redo, then undoes the rest. Write the journal first, the data later.",
 "Next video: result cache. Answers straight from memory. " + FOLLOW,
],
19: [
 "The same query runs ten thousand times a minute. Every time, Oracle reads the blocks and computes the answer again. Fifty milliseconds, over and over. What if Oracle just remembered the answer? That's the result cache.",
 "The buffer cache keeps data blocks. A query still has to read them, join, sort, and compute. The result cache, in the shared pool, keeps the finished result of a query or a function. Same query, same binds, same data? Oracle simply returns the stored answer.",
 "There are three ways to switch it on. The simplest: add the result cache hint to one query. Or annotate a table, so every query on it uses the cache automatically. Or mark a P L S Q L function with result cache. We'll see that one in a moment.",
 "The first execution finds nothing in the cache, so Oracle runs the query normally, and then stores the result. Every next execution with the same binds is a hit. No block reads, no execution. The answer comes straight from memory.",
 "Now someone updates the rate and commits. Oracle tracks which tables every cached result depends on, so it marks that result invalid. The next query recomputes it once, and caches the new answer. You never read a stale result.",
 "Mark a P L S Q L function as result cache, and Oracle caches its return value for each set of arguments. Call get rate for U S D a million times from a big query, and it really runs once per currency. And the cache is shared across all sessions, not just yours.",
 "So what belongs in the cache? Small, read-mostly lookups: exchange rates, branches, product codes, and summaries over stable data. What doesn't? Hot tables like transactions and balances, where every commit invalidates the cache. Huge results waste memory, and queries that never repeat never hit.",
 "Is it working? V dollar result cache objects shows each cached result, its status, and how often it was reused. A high scan count is great. Constantly invalid means that table doesn't belong there. Also check the statistics view, the memory report, and size the cache with result cache max size.",
 "Remember: the result cache stores final answers. The first run computes and stores, the next runs hit memory. A commit invalidates dependent results automatically. And it's best for small, read-mostly data.",
 "Next video: fragmentation and shrink. Why delete doesn't make a table smaller. " + FOLLOW,
],
20: [
 "You delete ninety percent of the rows in a ten gigabyte table. The table is still ten gigabytes, and the full scan takes exactly as long. Why? Meet the high water mark.",
 "A table segment is a set of blocks. Inserts fill them, one after another. The high water mark marks the highest block that was ever used. And a full table scan reads every single block below it. Remember that.",
 "Delete removes rows. The blocks become empty, but they still belong to the table, and the high water mark doesn't move. So a full scan still reads all those empty blocks. The same I O, for a fraction of the data.",
 "Truncate is different. It removes all rows at once and resets the high water mark to the start. Delete works row by row, with undo and redo for every row, and it can be rolled back. Truncate is D D L: fast, minimal undo, no rollback. Perfect for staging tables, but not when you need to keep some rows.",
 "To fix a table you want to keep, use shrink space. First enable row movement. Oracle moves rows from the end of the segment into free space at the start. Then it lowers the high water mark and releases the empty blocks, online, while users keep working. It needs automatic segment space management, and rowids change.",
 "Another option is alter table move. It copies the table into a brand new, packed segment, and drops the old one. Careful: rowids change, so a plain move leaves indexes unusable. Use the online option, or update indexes. And you need free space for a full copy. Afterwards, gather fresh statistics.",
 "The best fix is not to fragment at all. In banking, old transactions are purged every month. With delete, that leaves holes everywhere. Partition by date instead, and drop or truncate the old partition. It's instant, with no row by row undo and redo. And indexes? Only shrink or rebuild them when you have real evidence.",
 "How do you spot it? Compare the allocated size in D B A segments with the real data: rows times average row length. D B M S space shows how full the blocks are, and the segment advisor recommends what to shrink. A big gap plus slow full scans means a shrink candidate.",
 "Remember: delete removes rows, but the high water mark stays. Truncate resets it. Shrink space compacts online. Move rebuilds the segment, so check your indexes. And with partitions, drop, don't delete.",
 "Next video: A W R in three minutes. How to read your database's health report. " + FOLLOW,
],
}
